<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tekle Haymanot of Gojjam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1847–1901</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ras Adal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-saint">Tekle Haymanot</a>, earlier known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Adal, was ruler of Gojjam in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yohannes-iv">Yohannes IV</a> crowned him negus of Gojjam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> in 1881, but he was defeated by Menelik of Shewa at Embabo in 1882. He later fought the Mahdists and led his army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> in 1896. His son Ras Hailu succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tekle-haymanot-of-gojjam.jpg" alt="Tekle Haymanot of Gojjam" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tekle_Haymanot_of_Gojjam.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
